WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
A common mistake is to imagine that Bash simply sends a line of text directly to a program.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.640
That is not how the shell works.

3
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:20.140
Bash reads the script, parses shell syntax, performs expansions, sets up redirection and pipelines, and only then starts commands with an executable name and an argument list.

4
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.940
That order matters.

5
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:26.440
Variable expansion happens before the external command receives anything.

6
00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:30.700
Command substitution is evaluated before the surrounding command runs.

7
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.000
Word splitting can turn one expanded value into several words when it is not protected.

8
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:40.900
Globbing can turn pattern-like text into matching pathnames.

9
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.120
Redirection changes where input and output flow.

10
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.620
Pipelines connect commands and create their own failure-handling questions.

11
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.180
These features are powerful when they are used intentionally.

12
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:57.400
They let scripts compose tools, move data through filters, and automate repetitive work.

13
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:04.780
The risk appears when a script author forgets which parts of a command line Bash will interpret and which parts should remain literal.

14
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:13.760
A value that looks harmless as data can behave differently when the shell treats it as syntax, a pattern, an option, or a list of words.

15
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:20.040
For safe command execution, the key question is: what arguments will the target command actually receive?

16
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:29.260
The answer is not always obvious from the raw text if variables are unquoted, if command strings are assembled dynamically, or if a script relies on implicit shell behavior.

17
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.680
Defensive Bash code is written so this transformation is visible and controlled.

18
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.840
Exit status is part of execution as well.

19
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:42.380
Every command reports a result, and scripts need to decide whether that result is acceptable.

20
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:50.240
A failed lookup, missing file, rejected permission, or timed-out operation should not be hidden by later commands that appear to succeed.

21
00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.940
Understanding execution includes understanding both how commands start and how their results are interpreted.

22
00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:01.340
The practical takeaway is simple: Bash behavior happens before the tool sees its arguments.

23
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:13.920
Safe scripts protect values that should stay literal, avoid unnecessary dynamic interpretation, check results that affect state, and make pipelines and redirection explicit enough for a reviewer to understand.
